※ pl4g-test: compile=fail
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 9902 IMPL_UNIMPLEMENTED_FEATURE

※ Sets and dictionaries: written, typed and checked, and not yet built.  A
※ collection is a table somewhere in memory, and there is nowhere for one to
※ be -- the compiler emits no allocator and the language has no loop for a
※ lookup to walk.  Both are questions in the to-do lists, so what is here is
※ everything up to the point where those are answered.
※
※ `⸨a, b⸩` is a set and `⸨k: v⸩` a dictionary; which of the two a collection
※ is is decided by its first entry.  A type is written the same way: `⸨T⸩` and
※ `⸨K: V⸩`, so that a type and a value of it look alike.

enum Key { a ; b ; c }

※ a lookup in a set asks whether it holds the key
fn holds(s: ⸨u8⸩, k: u8) → bool:
    s⸨k⸩

※ and one in a dictionary answers with a result, so a key that is not there
※ cannot be read past by accident -- `??` says what to use instead
fn kept(d: ⸨Key: u8⸩, k: Key) → u8:
    d⸨k⸩ ?? 0u8

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    let s: ⸨u8⸩ = ⸨1u8, 2u8, 3u8⸩
    let d: ⸨Key: u8⸩ = ⸨Key.a: 1u8, Key.b: 2u8⸩
    ※ the four a set answers, written with the characters a number's bits use
    let joined: ⸨u8⸩ = s | ⸨4u8⸩
    let both: ⸨u8⸩ = s & ⸨1u8⸩
    let either: ⸨u8⸩ = s ^ ⸨1u8⸩
    let without: ⸨u8⸩ = s - ⸨1u8⸩
    ※ and an entry of a dictionary is assigned to where one is read
    d⸨Key.c⸩ ← 42u8
    0u8
